Instead of storing a stack, this version builds the answer one position at a time. If `keep` digits must remain, the next digit can only be chosen from a limited window. Pick the smallest digit in that window, then continue after it.

For `1432219` and `k = 3`, four digits must remain. The choices are `1`, `2`, `1`, and `9`, which makes `1219`.

The method does not change the input string. It is slower than the usual stack version, but the fixed-length subsequence idea is quite direct.

```java
class Solution {
    public String removeKdigits(String nums, int k) {
        int length = nums.length();
        if (k >= length) {
            return "0";
        }

        int keep = length - k;
        StringBuilder answer = new StringBuilder(keep);
        int start = 0;

        for (int position = 0; position < keep; position++) {
            int remaining = keep - position - 1;
            int end = length - remaining - 1;
            int best = start;

            for (int index = start + 1; index <= end; index++) {
                if (nums.charAt(index) < nums.charAt(best)) {
                    best = index;
                }
            }

            answer.append(nums.charAt(best));
            start = best + 1;
        }

        int first = 0;
        while (first < answer.length() && answer.charAt(first) == '0') {
            first++;
        }

        return first == answer.length() ? "0" : answer.substring(first);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case because each output position may scan many digits.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the returned builder.

A string can work as a stack here. When the next digit is smaller than the last saved digit, removing that last digit makes the number smaller, so use one deletion and pop it. Any deletions left after the scan are taken from the end.

Dry run for `num = "1432219"`, `k = 3`:
- `1`, `4` gives `14`
- `3` removes `4`, giving `13`
- the next `2` removes `3`, giving `12`
- the next `1` removes one `2`, giving `1219`

Leading zeroes are skipped at the end. The input string is passed by value, so the original value is not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string removeKdigits(string nums, int k) {
        if (k >= static_cast<int>(nums.size())) {
            return "0";
        }

        string kept;
        for (char digit : nums) {
            while (!kept.empty() && k > 0 && kept.back() > digit) {
                kept.pop_back();
                --k;
            }
            kept.push_back(digit);
        }

        while (k > 0 && !kept.empty()) {
            kept.pop_back();
            --k;
        }

        size_t first = kept.find_first_not_of('0');
        if (first == string::npos) {
            return "0";
        }
        return kept.substr(first);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, because every digit is added once and removed at most once.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the saved digits.

The input string itself can act like a stack. `kept` is the number of characters currently saved at the front of the string. When a larger saved digit is removed, decrease `kept`; when a new digit is kept, write it at that position.

This avoids a second stack container. It does modify the local copy of `nums`, but the platform caller still keeps its original string.

The final removal step trims the right side, which is correct when the digits never form a decreasing pair.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string removeKdigits(string nums, int k) {
        if (k >= static_cast<int>(nums.size())) {
            return "0";
        }

        int kept = 0;
        for (char digit : nums) {
            while (kept > 0 && k > 0 && nums[kept - 1] > digit) {
                --kept;
                --k;
            }
            nums[kept] = digit;
            ++kept;
        }

        kept -= k;
        int first = 0;
        while (first < kept && nums[first] == '0') {
            ++first;
        }

        if (first == kept) {
            return "0";
        }
        return nums.substr(first, kept - first);
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space, not counting the returned string.

